 in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL154@P3-4: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.Chapter 14: Polyominoes 363\nL155@P4: TABLE 14.3.1 The number of fixed, chiral, and\nL156@P4: free n-ominoes for n ≤ 24.\nL157@P4: n t(n) r(n) s(n)\nL158@P4: 1 1 1 1\nL159@P4: 2 2 1 1\nL160@P4: 3 6 2 2\nL161@P4: 4 19 7 5\nL162@P4: 5 63 18 12\nL163@P4: 6 216 60 35\nL164@P4: 7 760 196 108\nL165@P4: 8 2725 704 369\nL166@P4: 9 9910 2500 1285\nL167@P4: 10 36446 9189 4655\nL168@P4: 11 135268 33896 17073\nL169@P4: 12 505861 126759 63600\nL170@P4: 13 1903890 476270 238591\nL171@P4: 14 7204874 1802312 901971\nL172@P4: 15 27394666 6849777 3426576\nL173@P4: 16 104592937 26152418 13079255\nL174@P4: 17 400795844 100203194 50107909\nL175@P4: 18 1540820542 385221143 192622052\nL176@P4: 19 5940738676 1485200848 742624232\nL177@P4: 20 22964779660 5741256764 2870671950\nL178@P4: 21 88983512783 22245940545 11123060678\nL179@P4: 22 345532572678 86383382827 43191857688\nL252@P6: Can t(n) be computed in time polynomial with n?\nL253@P6: A related problem concerns the constant λ defined above:\nL254@P6: PROBLEM 14.3.4\nL255@P6: Is there a polynomial-time algorithm to find, for each n, an approximation λn of λ\nL256@P6: satisfying\nL257@P6: 10−n < |λn − λ| < 10−n+1 ?\nL258@P6: PROBLEM 14.3.5\nL259@P6: Define some decreasing sequence β = (β1, β2, . . .) that tends to λ, and give an\nL260@P6: algorithm to compute βn for every n.\nL261@P6: Define the two sequences τ1(n) = (t(n))1/n and τ2(n) = t(n+1)/t(n). A folklore\nL262@P6: polyomino-concatenation argument shows that for all n we have (t(n))2 ≤ t(2n),\nL263@P6: hence, (t(n))1/n ≤ (t(2n))1/(2n), which implies that (t(n))1/n ≤ λ for all n, that\nL264@P6: is, τ1(n) approaches λ from below (but is not necessarily monotone). However, it\nL265@P6: seems, given the first 56 elements of t(n), that both τ1(n) and τ2(n) are monotone\nL266@P6: increasing. This gives two more unsolved problems:\nL267@P6: PROBLEM 14.3.6\nL268@P6: Show that τ1(n) < τ1(n + 1) for all n.\nL269@P6: PROBLEM 14.3.7\nL270@P6: Show that τ2(n) < τ2(n + 1) for all n.\nL271@P6: 14.4 GENERATING POLYOMINOES\nL272@P6: The algorithm we describe to generate all n-ominoes, which is essentially due to\nL273@P6: Redelmeier [Red81], also provides a way of encoding n-ominoes. Starting with all\nL274@P6: n-ominoes in standard position, with each cell and each neighboring cell numbered,\nL275@P6: it constructs without repetitions all numbered (n+1)-ominoes in standard position.\nL276@P6: Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL277@P6-7: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.366 G. Barequet, S.W. Golomb, and D.A. Klarner\nL278@P7: GLOSSARY\nL279@P7: Border cell of an n-omino S: A cell [u, v], with v ≥ 0 or with v = 0 and u ≥ 0,\nL280@P7: adjacent to some cell of S. The set of all border cells, which is denoted by B(S),\nL281@P7: can be shown by induction to have no more than 2n elements.\nL282@P7: The algorithm, illustrated in Figure 14.4.1 for n = 1, 2, and 3, begins with\nL283@P7: cell 1 in position [0, 0], with its border cells marked 2 and 3, and then adds these—\nL284@P7: one at a time and in this order—each time numbering new border cells in their\nL285@P7: lexicographic order. Whenever a number used for a border cell is not larger than\nL286@P7: the largest internal number, it is circled, and the corresponding cell is not added\nL287@P7: at the next stage.\nL288@P7: FIGURE 14.4.1\nL289@P7: 1 2\nL290@P7: 3 5\nL291@P7: 1 2 4\nL292@P7: 6\nL293@P7: 4 3 5\nL294@P7: 1 2\nL295@P7: 7\nL296@P7: 6 3 5\nL297@P7: 1 2 4\nL298@P7: 3 5 7\nL299@P7: 2 4 6\nL300@P7: 7\nL301@P7: 3 5 6\nL302@P7: 1 2 4\nL303@P7: 8 6\nL304@P7: 7 4 3 5\nL305@P7: 1 2\nL306@P7: 6 8\nL307@P7: 4 3 5 7\nL308@P7: 1 2\nL309@P7: 9\nL310@P7: 7 6 8\nL311@P7: 4 3 5\nL312@P7: 1 1 2\nL313@P7: 3\nL314@P7: Figure 14.4.2 shows all the 4-ominoes produced in this way, with their border\nL315@P7: cells marked for the next step of the algorithm.\nL316@P7: This process assigns a unique set of positive integers to each n-omino S, also\nL317@P7: illustrated in Figure 14.4.2. The set character functions for these integer sets, in\nL318@P7: turn, truncated after their final 1’s, provide a binary codeword χ(S) for each n\u0002omino S. For example, the code words for the first three 4-ominoes in Figure 14.4.2\nL319@P7: would be 1111, 11101, and 111001.\nL320@P7: PROBLEM 14.4.1\nL321@P7: Which binary strings arise as codewords for n-ominoes?\nL322@P7: The following is easy to see:\nL323@P7: THEOREM 14.4.2\nL1117@P21: [Mer90] S. Mertens. Lattice animals: A fast enumeration algorithm and new perimeter poly\u0002nomials. J. Stat. Phys., 58:1095–1108, 1990.\nL1118@P21: [ML92] S. Mertens and M.E. Lautenbacher. Counting lattice animals: A parallel attack. J.\nL1119@P21: Stat. Phys., 66:669–678, 1992.\nL1120@P21: [oeis] The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences. Published electronically at\nL1121@P21: http://oeis.org .\nL1122@P21: [PG95] P.J. Peard and D.S. Gaunt. 1/d-expansions for the free energy of lattice animal models\nL1123@P21: of a self-interacting branched polymer. J. Phys. A: Math. Gen., 28:6109–6124, 1995.\nL1124@P21: [Rea62] R.C. Read. Contributions to the cell growth problem. Canad. J. Math., 14:1–20, 1962.\nL1125@P21: [Red81] D.H. Redelmeier Counting polyominoes: Yet another attack. Discrete Math., 36:191–\nL1126@P21: 203, 1981.\nL1127@P21: [Rei05] M. Reid. Klarner systems and tiling boxes with polyominoes. J. Combin. Theory, Ser.\nL1128@P21: A, 111:89–105, 2005.\nL1129@P21: [RW89] E.J.J. van Rensburg and S.G. Whittington. Self-avoiding surfaces. J. Phys. A: Math.\nL1130@P21: Gen., 22:4939–4958, 1989.\nL1131@P21: [SW92] I. Stewart and A. Wormstein. Polyominoes of order 3 do not exist. J. Combin. Theory,\nL1132@P21: Ser. A, 61:130–136, 1992.\nL1133@P21: [Van98] C. Vanderzande. Lattice Models of Polymers. Cambridge University Press, 1998.\nL1134@P21: [VG03] M. V¨oge and A.J. Guttmann. On the number of hexagonal polyominoes. Theoret.\nL1135@P21: Comput. Sci., 307:433–453, 2003.\nL1136@P21: [Yan14] J. Yang. Rectangular tileability and complementary tileability are undecidable. Euro\u0002pean J. Combin., 41:20–34, 2014.\nL1137@P21: Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL1138@P21: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.csun.edu/~ctoth/Handbook/chap14.pdf)\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 22; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn5view0\",\"pattern\":\"Redelmeier\"}); Total lines: 1139\nL125@P2: Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL126@P2-3: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.362 G. Barequet, S.W. Golomb, and D.A. Klarner\nL127@P3: FIGURE 14.2.4\nL128@P3: The twelve free 5-ominoes.\nL129@P3: FIGURE 14.2.5\nL130@P3: A set of n\nL131@P3: 2 −n+ 1 cells (for n = 5) that contains\nL132@P3: every n-omino in standard position.\nL133@P3: x\nL134@P3: y\nL135@P3: (Some of these instruction sets are illegal, and some other sets produce the same\nL136@P3: polyominoes.) Hence, the number of polyominoes of size n is less than\nL137@P3: 3(n−1)\nL138@P3: n−1\nL139@P3: \u0001\nL140@P3: .\nL141@P3: 14.3 HOW MANY n-OMINOES ARE THERE?\nL142@P3: Table 14.3.1, calculated by Redelmeier [Red81], indicates the values of t(n), r(n),\nL143@P3: and s(n) for n = 1, . . . , 24. Jensen and Guttmann [JG00, Jen01] and Jensen [Jen03]\nL144@P3: extended the enumeration of polyominoes up to n = 56. See also sequence A001168\nL145@P3: in the OEIS (On-line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences) [oeis].\nL146@P3: It is easy to see that for each n, we have\nL147@P3: t(n)\nL148@P3: 8\nL149@P3: ≤ s(n) ≤ r(n) ≤ t(n).\nL150@P3: The values of t(n) seem to be growing exponentially, and indeed they have expo\u0002nential bounds.\nL151@P3: THEOREM 14.3.1 [Kla67]\nL152@P3: limn→∞(t(n))1/n = λ exists.\nL153@P3: Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL154@P3-4: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.Chapter 14: Polyominoes 363\nL155@P4: TABLE 14.3.1 The number of fixed, chiral, and\nL156@P4: free n-ominoes for n ≤ 24.\nL157@P4: n t(n) r(n) s(n)\nL158@P4: 1 1 1 1\nL159@P4: 2 2 1 1\nL160@P4: 3 6 2 2\nL161@P4: 4 19 7 5\nL162@P4: 5 63 18 12\nL163@P4: 6 216 60 35\nL164@P4: 7 760 196 108\nL165@P4: 8 2725 704 369\nL166@P4: 9 9910 2500 1285\nL167@P4: 10 36446 9189 4655\nL168@P4: 11 135268 33896 17073\nL169@P4: 12 505861 126759 63600\nL170@P4: 13 1903890 476270 238591\nL171@P4: 14 7204874 1802312 901971\nL172@P4: 15 27394666 6849777 3426576\nL173@P4: 16 104592937 26152418 13079255\nL174@P4: 17 400795844 100203194 50107909\nL175@P4: 18 1540820542 385221143 192622052\nL176@P4: 19 5940738676 1485200848 742624232\nL177@P4: 20 22964779660 5741256764 2870671950\nL178@P4: 21 88983512783 22245940545 11123060678\nL179@P4: 22 345532572678 86383382827 43191857688\nL180@P4: 23 1344372335524 336093325058 168047007728\nL181@P4: 24 5239988770268 1309998125640 654999700403\nL182@P4: This constant (often referred to in the literature as the “growth constant” of\nL183@P4: polyominoes) has since then been called “Klarner’s constant.” Only three decades\nL184@P4: later, Madras proved the existence of the asymptotic growth ratio.\nL185@P4: THEOREM 14.3.2 [Mad99]\nL186@P4: limn→∞ t(n + 1)/t(n) exists (and is hence equal to λ).\nL187@P4: The currently best known lower [BRS16] and upper [KR73] bounds on the\nL188@P4: constant λ are 4.0025 and 4.6496, respectively. The proof of the lower bound uses an\nL189@P4: analysis of polyominoes on twisted cylinders with the help of a supercomputer, and\nL190@P4: the proof of the upper bound uses a composition argument of so-called twigs. The\nL191@P4: currently best (unproved) estimate of λ, 4.0625696±0.0000005, is by Jensen [Jen03].\nL192@P4: ALGORITHMS\nL193@P4: Redelmeier\nL194@P4: Considerable effort has been expended to find a formula for the number of fixed n\u0002ominoes (say), with no success. Redelmeier’s algorithm, which produced the entries\nL195@P4: in Table 14.3.1, generates all fixed n-ominoes one by one and counts them. The\nL196@P4: Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL197@P4-5: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.364 G. Barequet, S.W. Golomb, and D.A. Klarner\nL198@P5: recursive algorithm searches G, the underlying cell-adjacency graph of the square\nL199@P5: lattice, and counts all connected subgraphs of G (up to a predetermined size) that\nL200@P5: contain some canonical vertex, say, (0, 0), which is assumed to always correspond\nL201@P5: to the leftmost cell in the bottom row of the polyomino. (This prevents multiple\nL202@P5: counting of translations of the same polyomino.) See more details in Section 14.4.\nL203@P5: Although the running time of the algorithm is necessarily exponential, it takes only\nL204@P5: O(n) space. Redelmeier’s algorithm was extended to other lattices, parallelized,\nL205@P5: and enhanced further; see [AB09a, AB09b, LM11, Mer90, ML92].\nL206@P5: Jensen\nL207@P5: A faster transfer-matrix algorithm for counting polyominoes was described by Jensen\nL208@P5: [Jen03], but its running time is still exponential in the size of counted polyominoes.\nL209@P5: This algorithm does not produce all polyominoes. Instead, it maintains all possible\nL210@P5: polyomino boundaries (see Figure 14.3.1), which are the possible configurations of\nL211@P5: FIGURE 14.3.1\nL212@P5: A partially built 21-cell polyomino with boundary -A-BA-C.\nL213@P5: q\nL214@P5: −−\nL215@P5: −−\nL216@P5: −−\nL217@P5: B\nL218@P5: A\nL219@P5: C\nL220@P5: A\nL221@P5: the right sides of the polyominoes, associated with information about the connec\u0002tivity between cells of the boundary through polyomino cells found to the left of\nL222@P5: the boundary. Polyominoes are “built” column by column from left to right, and\nL223@P5: in every column, cells are considered from top to bottom. The algorithm maintains\nL224@P5: a database indexed by the boundaries, where for each possible boundary σ, the\nL225@P5: database keeps only the counts of “partial” polyominoes of all possible sizes, which\nL226@P5: can have σ as their boundary. (“Partial” in the sense that polyominoes may still\nL227@P5: be invalid in this stage due to having more than one component, but they may\nL228@P5: become valid (connected) later when more cells are added to the polyomino.) The\nL229@P5: figure shows a polyomino with the boundary -A-BA-C, where letters represent com\u0002ponents of the polyominoes corresponding to this boundary. When considering the\nL230@P5: next cell q (just below the “kink” in the boundary), there are two options: either\nL231@P5: to add q to the polyomino, or to not add it. Choosing either option will change\nL232@P5: the boundary from σ to σ\nL233@P5: ′\nL234@P5: (possibly σ\nL235@P5: ′ = σ), and the algorithm will then update\nL236@P5: the contents of the entry with index σ\nL237@P5: ′\nL238@P5: in the database. (Note again that the\nL239@P5: database does not keep the polyominoes, but only counts of polyominoes for each\nL240@P5: possible boundary.) The algorithm counts all connected polyominoes produced in\nL241@P5: this process, up to a prescribed size, and for all possible heights (7 in the figure).\nL242@P5: Analysis of the algorithm [BM07] reveals that the major factor that influences the\nL243@P5: performance of the algorithm (in terms of both running time and memory con\u0002Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL244@P5-6: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.Chapter 14: Polyominoes 365\nL245@P6: sumption) is the number of possible boundaries. It turns out that the number of\nL246@P6: possible boundaries of length b is proportional to 3b, up to a small polynomial fac\u0002tor. Due to symmetry, the algorithm needs to consider boundaries of length up to\nL247@P6: only ⌈n/2⌉ for counting polyominoes of size n. Hence, the time complexity of the\nL248@P6: algorithm is roughly 3n/2 ≈ 1.73n, which is significantly less than the total number\nL249@P6: of polyominoes (about 4.06n).\nL250@P6: UNSOLVED PROBLEMS\nL251@P6: PROBLEM 14.3.3\nL252@P6: Can t(n) be computed in time polynomial with n?\nL253@P6: A related problem concerns the constant λ defined above:\nL254@P6: PROBLEM 14.3.4\nL255@P6: Is there a polynomial-time algorithm to find, for each n, an approximation λn of λ\nL256@P6: satisfying\nL257@P6: 10−n < |λn − λ| < 10−n+1 ?\nL258@P6: PROBLEM 14.3.5\nL259@P6: Define some decreasing sequence β = (β1, β2, . . .) that tends to λ, and give an\nL260@P6: algorithm to compute βn for every n.\nL261@P6: Define the two sequences τ1(n) = (t(n))1/n and τ2(n) = t(n+1)/t(n). A folklore\nL262@P6: polyomino-concatenation argument shows that for all n we have (t(n))2 ≤ t(2n),\nL263@P6: hence, (t(n))1/n ≤ (t(2n))1/(2n), which implies that (t(n))1/n ≤ λ for all n, that\nL264@P6: is, τ1(n) approaches λ from below (but is not necessarily monotone). However, it\nL265@P6: seems, given the first 56 elements of t(n), that both τ1(n) and τ2(n) are monotone\nL266@P6: increasing. This gives two more unsolved problems:\nL267@P6: PROBLEM 14.3.6\nL268@P6: Show that τ1(n) < τ1(n + 1) for all n.\nL269@P6: PROBLEM 14.3.7\nL270@P6: Show that τ2(n) < τ2(n + 1) for all n.\nL271@P6: 14.4 GENERATING POLYOMINOES\nL272@P6: The algorithm we describe to generate all n-ominoes, which is essentially due to\nL273@P6: Redelmeier [Red81], also provides a way of encoding n-ominoes. Starting with all\nL274@P6: n-ominoes in standard position, with each cell and each neighboring cell numbered,\nL275@P6: it constructs without repetitions all numbered (n+1)-ominoes in standard position.\nL276@P6: Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL277@P6-7: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.366 G. Barequet, S.W. Golomb, and D.A. Klarner\nL278@P7: GLOSSARY\nL279@P7: Border cell of an n-omino S: A cell [u, v], with v ≥ 0 or with v = 0 and u ≥ 0,\nL280@P7: adjacent to some cell of S. The set of all border cells, which is denoted by B(S),\nL281@P7: can be shown by induction to have no more than 2n elements.\nL282@P7: The algorithm, illustrated in Figure 14.4.1 for n = 1, 2, and 3, begins with\nL283@P7: cell 1 in position [0, 0], with its border cells marked 2 and 3, and then adds these—\nL284@P7: one at a time and in this order—each time numbering new border cells in their\nL285@P7: lexicographic order. Whenever a number used for a border cell is not larger than\nL286@P7: the largest internal number, it is circled, and the corresponding cell is not added\nL287@P7: at the next stage.\nL288@P7: FIGURE 14.4.1\nL289@P7: 1 2\nL290@P7: 3 5\nL291@P7: 1 2 4\nL292@P7: 6\nL293@P7: 4 3 5\nL294@P7: 1 2\nL295@P7: 7\nL296@P7: 6 3 5\nL297@P7: 1 2 4\nL298@P7: 3 5 7\nL299@P7: 2 4 6\nL300@P7: 7\nL301@P7: 3 5 6\nL302@P7: 1 2 4\nL303@P7: 8 6\nL304@P7: 7 4 3 5\nL305@P7: 1 2\nL306@P7: 6 8\nL307@P7: 4 3 5 7\nL308@P7: 1 2\nL309@P7: 9\nL310@P7: 7 6 8\nL311@P7: 4 3 5\nL312@P7: 1 1 2\nL313@P7: 3\nL314@P7: Figure 14.4.2 shows all the 4-ominoes produced in this way, with their border\nL315@P7: cells marked for the next step of the algorithm.\nL316@P7: This process assigns a unique set of positive integers to each n-omino S, also\nL317@P7: illustrated in Figure 14.4.2. The set character functions for these integer sets, in\nL318@P7: turn, truncated after their final 1’s, provide a binary codeword χ(S) for each n\u0002omino S. For example, the code words for the first three 4-ominoes in Figure 14.4.2\nL319@P7: would be 1111, 11101, and 111001.\nL895@P17: and A151830–35 for counts of polycubes in dimensions 3 through 9, respectively,\nL896@P17: in the OEIS [oeis]. The most comprehensive counts to date were obtained by a\nL897@P17: parallel version of Redelmeier’s algorithm adapted to dimensions greater than 2.\nL898@P17: Similarly to two dimensions, let λd denote the growth constant of polycubes in\nL899@P17: d dimensions. It was proven [BBR10] that λd = 2ed − o(d), where e is the base of\nL900@P17: natural logarithms, and evidence shows that λd ∼ (2d − 3)e + O(1/d) as d → ∞.\nL1123@P21: of a self-interacting branched polymer. J. Phys. A: Math. Gen., 28:6109–6124, 1995.\nL1124@P21: [Rea62] R.C. Read. Contributions to the cell growth problem. Canad. J. Math., 14:1–20, 1962.\nL1125@P21: [Red81] D.H. Redelmeier Counting polyominoes: Yet another attack. Discrete Math., 36:191–\nL1126@P21: 203, 1981.\nL1127@P21: [Rei05] M. Reid. Klarner systems and tiling boxes with polyominoes. J. Combin. Theory, Ser.\nL1128@P21: A, 111:89–105, 2005.\nL1129@P21: [RW89] E.J.J. van Rensburg and S.G. Whittington. Self-avoiding surfaces. J. Phys. A: Math.\n"